                <p>
                    <span
                        class="tag"
                        data-emoji="🌵"
                        onclick="floatEmoji(this)"
                    >
                        Cacti
                    </span>{" "}
                    or short-lived{" "}
                    <span
                        class="tag"
                        data-emoji="🦋"
                        onclick="floatEmoji(this)"
                    >
                        butterflies
                    </span>{" "}
                    even bright & muddy plastic{" "}
                    <span
                        class="tag"
                        data-emoji="☣️"
                        onclick="floatEmoji(this)"
                    >
                        pesticide
                    </span>{" "}
                    containers! Is digital data
                </p>
                <p>
                                        <span
                        class="tag"
                        data-emoji="🎭"
                        onclick="floatEmoji(this)"
                    >Dada</span>? Or is the screen too <span
                        class="tag"
                        data-emoji="🪢"
                        onclick="floatEmoji(this)"
                    >braided</span> up with <span
                        class="tag"
                        data-emoji="☮️"
                        onclick="floatEmoji(this)"
                    >symbolism</span>? Plus
                    the hazards, like click a rifle-laden
                </p>
                <p>
                    <span
                        class="tag"
                        data-emoji="🦅"
                        onclick="floatEmoji(this)"
                    >Eagle</span> & maybe it’s downloading an historical evil or
                    gambling app. So this is where the
                </p>
                <p>
                    Fables live now & art, more interconnected & perhaps less
                    fragile than before. Available for
                </p>
                <p>
                    Gangs of corporate bots & aunts, grandmothers, brothers. Too
                    expansive for a
                </p>
                <p>
                    Haibun but glossed with standardization. Hand lettering
                    leaving; each word’s a god again like mint
                </p>
                <p>
                    Ice cream in the Tucson heat & you can eat it! What longings
                    are left in the whitespaces or
                </p>
                <p>Jank dead blogs by midwest teens? We won’t say</p>
                <p>
                    Kaddish for some lost bytes. This is a poem for dynamic
                    data, still evolving, deep listening
                </p>
                <p>Labor of attention! Here is meditation attuned to</p>
                <p>Macaronic expression, to metaphors, to meter. To find a</p>
                <p>
                    Name for every category’s a parable of minor power– the font
                    suggests
                </p>
                <p>Objectivity but this heap of words is romance.</p>
                <p>
                    Pain and all of it. My own computer heats & whirs when I
                    listen too long. A
                </p>
                <p>
                    Q and A on the Apple forum tells me I’ve abused my storage.
                    Outside
                </p>
                <p>
                    Rabbits run around the creosote. I’m headphones in 2001 yes
                    there’s
                </p>
                <p>
                    Sadness & two sets of poems depending on september’s
                    capitalization. Were there
                </p>
                <p>
                    Tabloids stealing grief? I was too young & still don’t know
                    proper
                </p>
                <p>
                    Ultratranslation to layer past & present. The poets don’t
                    say who sold
                </p>
                <p>
                    Vacations for survivors. I’ll leave the rabbits and the
                    creosote. The audio
                </p>
                <p>
                    Waffles. My technology’s decaying but I’m still building
                    data. More
                </p>
                <p>
                    eXactly: something’s building mine & someone’s harvesting &
                    maybe hoping
                </p>
                <p>
                    Young readers won’t learn scaffolding if the structure’s
                    smooth enough. But there was a lone
                </p>
                <p>Zebra in the local zoo & she hasn’t been forgotten.</p>
            </div>

            <p>
                Note: Alysa also wrote some metapoetry. It can be found in the
                blog <a href="/visualizing-voca/blog/alysa-metapoetry">here</a>.
                We chose to exclude it from this essay to focus on making poems
                from the metadata.
            </p>
        </main>
        <Footer />
    </body>
</html>

<script>
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-emoji]").forEach((el) => {
        el.addEventListener("click", () => {
            el.classList.remove("float");
            void el.offsetWidth;
            el.classList.add("float");
        });
    });
</script>
